WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.740
- I'm really excited to introduce
my colleague, Jim Scott,

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.480
who is a graduate of the
Moorestown Friends School

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.690
in New Jersey, as well
as other fine schools.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.740
He's the author of
several important books,

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.390
some of which you have probably heard of.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.310
I'd call the books classics,
but unlike classics,

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.860
some people actually
enjoy reading Jim's books.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:31.100
He's a sterling professor of
political science here at Yale

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.510
but we in anthropology
really appreciate it

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.630
when he calls himself an
honorary anthropologist.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.700
And we in the Southeast
Asian Studies Council

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.400
really need to thank Jim
for the mountains of merit

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.750
that he has accrued on
our behalf over the years.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.520
In Burma, like other parts
of Theravada Southeast Asia,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.280
merit making entails the act of giving,

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.530
cultivating virtue and mental development.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.170
And I think it suffices to say

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.490
that Jim does all of those things.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.230
He's a man of many good
deeds and much merit

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.490
and it's a real pleasure
to introduce him today

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.130
for his talk on Irrawaddy.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.593
So Jim, take it away.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.190
- Thanks a lot, Eric, for
that generous introduction.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.320
I wanna begin actually, by noticing

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.180
as you all have perhaps noticed yourselves

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.830
that we are well into our second week

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:27.863
of the military coup in Burma.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.760
And there are a whole series of efforts

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.110
that are underway to provide
some international support

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.173
for the nonviolent resistance in Burma.

00:01:47.519 --> 00:01:49.790
There are gonna be other opportunities

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.590
including efforts to
raise subsistence funds

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.880
for all the doctors and teachers

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.993
and civil servants who were on strike.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.650
And I have contributed actually
a good part of my royalties

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.380
for the last 10 years to
the Assistance Association

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.530
for Political Prisoners in Burma.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.870
It's an association of ex prisoners

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:15.870
that takes care of prisoners
by sending them food,

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.310
medicines, taking care of their families

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:23.173
and taking care of them when
and if they're released.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:27.933
And so I think Chris will
put up the easiest way

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.800
to contribute is via the
campaign for the Rohingya

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.280
in which the money would be forwarded

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.220
to the Assistance Association
for Political Prisoners.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.960
They provide, by the way

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.207
if you go to their website, AAPP(Burma),

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.120
they provide a daily briefing

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.770
on what's happening in Burma now.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.980
And I recommend that as a source
for day-to-day information

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.683
on the current situation
in Burma, so thank you.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.623
And I'll move now to my talk if I may.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.363
The talk is called in praise of floods.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.970
I've been teaching a seminar on rivers

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.600
called rivers nature and politics

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.260
for the last seven or eight years.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.740
And historically I've done
actually a fair amount

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.150
of canoeing and fishing on American rivers

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.470
and spent a long time on
the Irrawaddy in Burma

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.270
until the pandemic.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:40.890
And in fact, although I was
interested in the river itself,

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.950
the river was also partly an excuse for me

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.930
that I wanted to make sure that no matter

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.280
where I was in Burma

00:03:48.182 --> 00:03:50.770
that if the police asked what I was doing

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:53.070
I could say I was studying the Irrawaddy

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:56.342
because I would always
be close to a tributary

00:03:56.342 --> 00:03:57.680
or distributary of the Irrawaddy

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.260
no matter where I was in Burma

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:03.180
although that would not
hold if I were in Kenton

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.760
in this sort of Eastern (indistinct)

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.400
But I date germ of this talk actually

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.400
to an encounter I had
something like 20 years ago.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:21.180
I found myself at a conference

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.450
in which there were hydrologists
and Southeast Asians

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.240
the two separate meetings going on

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.120
but we were all at the
same conference site

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:35.120
and they wanted us to be good
companions to one another.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.000
So they ordered us at lunch and dinner

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.830
to intersperse ourselves
and carry on conversations

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:45.223
across disciplines and interests.

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:50.910
And I found myself sitting
next to a very smart

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:53.340
and interesting Philippine hydrologist

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.680
and trying to be a good boy
and make conversation with him.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.460
I tried to think, well,
what can we talk about?

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.820
And I had learned in the
last two or three years

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.610
before this meeting
that the Colorado River

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.410
never got to the sea for
a large part of the year.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:17.163
And I was sad for the Colorado River,

00:05:17.163 --> 00:05:19.380
it seemed to me all our
metaphors about rivers

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:24.380
running to the sea, that it
just gave me a sense of sympathy

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.900
for the Colorado, that it
didn't get to do its thing

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.920
and run to the sea,
like all rivers should.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.060
And so I tried to start conversation

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.760
by saying to the hydrologists
that I learned this

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:41.010
about the Colorado wasn't too bad,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.420
that Colorado didn't get to the sea.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:48.420
And he turned to me very quickly
and with a stentorian voice

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:51.970
and said, no, no, it's
the most wonderful thing

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.520
in the world, it means that
every drop of the Colorado River

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.840
is used for some important purpose

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.100
and not a drop goes to waste.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:03.140
And I realized from that
moment, we were probably

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:04.910
not gonna have a long conversation

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:07.783
because he was if you like,

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.900
interested in the functionality

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.140
for homo sapiens of the river and the uses

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.293
to which it could be put.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.600
He was not alone,

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:26.270
and here, I want to
quote Winston Churchill

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:29.840
on the the Nile River.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:34.300
And I quote, "One day
every last drop of water,

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.640
which drains into the
whole valley of the Nile

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.720
shall be equally and amicably divided

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.640
among the river people

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.080
and the Nile itself
shall perish gloriously

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.607
and never reach the sea."

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:55.990
Joseph Stalin, who was less
given to ornamental pros,

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:57.163
put it more succinctly,

00:06:58.749 --> 00:07:02.047
"Water which is allowed to
enter the sea is wasted."

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.480
You could have had similar
things spoken by anyone

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:12.550
from the American Bureau of Reclamation

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:17.550
or the Army Corps of Engineers
until very, very recently.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:23.133
I want to begin actually with assertion

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:30.107
that everything moves
and rivers too of course,

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:36.110
but it seems to me that
one of the problems

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:38.760
is that humans are inclined

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.243
to thinking of units over human life.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.880
And we don't think of river
time, of mountain time,

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:50.410
of truly time and so on,

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:54.980
let alone smaller units like
insect time, you might say,

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:59.860
but if you open the
temporal lens wide enough,

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:01.180
everything moves.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:06.180
And my illustration of this,
let's see if this works,

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:15.603
oh, there we go.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:24.710
This is 120,000 year video of the movement

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:27.803
of the polar ice cap.

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:37.083
You can see it moving.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:44.680
And how many thousand years ago is up

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:46.303
in the upper left-hand corner?

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:51.000
You will see that 17 or 18,000 years ago

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:56.000
was the last Glacial
Maximum after which the ice,

00:08:57.430 --> 00:08:59.580
the polar cap retreats

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.873
and continues retreating today, of course.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:09.053
But you see in the enormous
variation over time.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:20.060
Rivers, like the St. Lawrence Seaway,

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:23.400
were essentially the
breaking up of a glacial Lake

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:28.340
called Lake Agassi right after
the last Glacial Maximum.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:30.290
So now that's the last Glacial Maximum,

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:34.653
and then there's the retreat.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.370
The picture I would have
preferred to show you

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.450
although I couldn't get an animation of it

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.300
is that after the last Glacial Maximum,

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:47.380
we think of trees for
example, as being static

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:49.310
and being not moving.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:50.917
But in fact, they're moving all the time

00:09:50.917 --> 00:09:53.710
and it's again opening the temporal lens.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:57.910
So if you were to have a
time elapsed photograph

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:00.940
after the last Glacial Maximum,

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:05.120
you would see century by
century, the oaks and the beaches

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.250
moving North from the Mediterranean

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:11.620
as the climate improved
and things got warmer.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:13.670
And so the trees would be moving

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.540
and bringing together
with them their soils,

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:20.080
the insects, the animals and so on

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.080
that are part of the larger
ecosystem of arboreal forest.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:32.647
I want to next move to, there we go,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:37.160
these are actually prepared by
the Army Corps of Engineers.

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:42.120
This is about a thousand year depiction

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.090
of the different bends and channels

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:46.890
of the Mississippi River.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:51.447
The white portion is the current channel

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:55.372
of the Mississippi River,

00:10:55.372 --> 00:11:00.372
but you get some sense for
where the Mississippi has been

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:03.540
and some sense for where it might go,

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:04.740
were it not(indistinct).

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:08.970
This is another version of the river,

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.473
I think near St. Louis,
if I'm not mistaken.

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:18.160
The other example that's quite famous,

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:22.120
is the Yellow River estuary.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:24.870
There are major shifts North and South

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:29.563
of the Shandong Peninsula from 2200 BC.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:34.690
And the second slide shows shifts

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:39.310
in the major distributaries
of the Yellow River

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:41.823
in the last two centuries alone.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:44.973
It's a huge variation,

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:50.320
and in fact, the second to last movement

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:54.980
of the major channel of
the Yellow River to sea

00:11:56.210 --> 00:12:01.210
was in 1938 when Chiang
Kai-shek broke the dykes

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:05.270
of the Yellow River in
order to send it South

00:12:05.270 --> 00:12:09.450
in order to impede the
Japanese advance to the North.

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:12.470
It was successful, but
it cost 300,000 lives.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:21.670
The process of this change has all to do

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:25.473
with the gradient of the coastal plain.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:30.523
That is to say, if the
gradient is very low,

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.980
the river slows down and as it slows,

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:38.980
the sedimentaries settles
out and raises the river bed.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:41.490
Over time itself damming,

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:45.950
that is to say it creates
its own levee ahead of it

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:48.210
and then it spreads laterally

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.123
and seeks another path to the sea.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:53.380
As a new channels cloud over time,

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:55.730
the river may go back to an older channel

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:58.340
that is now a faster way to the sea.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:01.560
The process is natural,
though the amount of sediment

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.880
can vastly be increased of course,

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:07.070
by deforestation in the watershed.

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:10.250
And of course the river is
likely to build new land

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:11.863
in the estuary itself.

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.510
Each flood raises the land flooded

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:17.010
by the deposition of sediment

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:21.330
so that the floodplain of
the middle mile, for example,

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:25.363
is now 10 feet higher than
it was 5,000 years ago.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:29.310
George Perkins Marsh actually
understood all of this

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:34.310
in his magnificent book, "Man
and Nature" published in 1864.

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:39.060
Thus river port towns and coastal towns

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:41.890
that are river's mouth or
vulnerable to being blocked

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:44.320
by the buildup of silt and sand.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:48.060
The most famous example
of this, I think is Bruges,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:52.265
the great linen center
it's river, the artery

00:13:52.265 --> 00:13:55.040
and the artery that made it Bruges

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:57.930
as a linen center was the Zwijn River.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:13:59.990
It's silted up in the 15th century

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:02.500
and the merchants left for Antwerp

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:05.569
leaving this urban 16th century gem

00:14:05.569 --> 00:14:10.569
as a kind of museum piece
that we can admire today.

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:13.410
On the Upper Rhine for
example, (indistinct)

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:15.310
in Roman times it was on the left bank

00:14:16.228 --> 00:14:19.590
and the 10th century it was on an island,

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:22.200
in the 13th century on
the left bank again,

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.820
and in the 14th century up until today,

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:26.593
it was on the right bank.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:32.990
So this movement is constant over time

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:37.550
and in my personal experience,
we have a summer cabin

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:42.010
in Central Pennsylvania, which
we would go to every summer

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:45.610
for the better part of a
decade with the children.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:50.530
And I observed each year, the
what's called the accretion

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:53.976
or carving out by the ice in the spring

00:14:53.976 --> 00:14:58.976
of each meander and had the
sense for the slow change

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:01.940
in the course of the river.

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:06.817
But I was misled by this
gradual change because in 1972

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:09.313
there was a huge flood.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:15.310
I realized that 99% of
the change in this Creek

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:18.610
came during the high water
of this massive flood,

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.600
two or three hours, right in 1972

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:26.040
that cut off meanders,
leaving oxbows and so on

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:29.830
and that this is called in
international law avulsion

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.173
rather than accretion.

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:35.140
And that most of the
great changes in the river

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:40.140
are these catastrophic events
of massive high water moments.

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:46.447
I want to show, this is the
process of creating oxbows.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.770
This is an oxbow that
has been recently cut off

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:05.210
as you can see.

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:07.880
And the next flood you can see an oxbow

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:11.495
that whose lifetime is limited probably

00:16:11.495 --> 00:16:15.940
to the next great flood,
because that loop to your left

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.950
will be cut off as well.

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:23.630
I'm going to leave up
of the Irrawaddy River

00:16:26.259 --> 00:16:30.020
Which its major tributaries to the North

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:34.370
of the Malikha and Maika that join

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:37.890
to create the Irrawaddy North of Myitkyina

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:44.120
that then flows South Mandalay,
it's joined by the Chindwin

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:50.480
and if I had a map of
the Irrawaddy over time

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:54.590
it's been changed radically,

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:58.000
it's channeled by volcanic events.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:01.460
The Sittaung River was in fact the channel

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:06.460
of the Irrawaddy to the
sea in geological times

00:17:06.570 --> 00:17:10.469
and after the eruption of
Mount Popa and (indistinct)

00:17:10.469 --> 00:17:13.883
the bank of the Irrawaddy
was changed quite radically.

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:18.660
The Irrawaddy is moving all the time,

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.233
and I want to give you a sense of this.

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:24.450
On one trip downstream, from Mandalay

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:29.300
to the ancient capital of
Bagan, it was a day's trip

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:33.200
a small inboard motor boat

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:35.943
carrying no more than 20 passengers.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:42.260
What was interesting to me
is that we used four pilots

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:44.810
whom we picked up along the way,

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:47.850
and each of these pilots was an expert

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:50.440
in a section of the river

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:55.223
and essentially a two
hour sail down the river.

00:17:56.370 --> 00:18:00.880
And after their knowledge
ran out, they would get off

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:04.680
and we would take on another
pilot who was an expert

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:07.012
for the next little patch of the river

00:18:07.012 --> 00:18:11.530
and these people had worked at
these jobs for 20, 30 years.

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:14.390
So the captain of the
boat who've been going

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:16.780
up and down the Irrawaddy for a long time,

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:21.560
he did not trust himself to
understand the different shifts

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:27.826
in the sandbanks and channel
day by day and week by week,

00:18:27.826 --> 00:18:30.360
without using these pilots.

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:33.630
And still in this case,
we didn't get stuck,

00:18:33.630 --> 00:18:38.210
but in many cases, even with these pilots

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:43.250
it's not very uncommon to be stuck.

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:45.420
And the larger cargo carriers

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:49.760
especially during low
water in February and March

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:54.280
the danger was much greater than many

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:58.700
of the larger cargo carriers
that are irretrievably stuck

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:01.300
and have to wait until the monsoon season

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:06.030
in order to be floated off the mud banks

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:10.683
or sandbanks in which they're embedded.

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:17.933
Next, I want to show,

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:24.120
this is a common site on some
of the larger passenger boats.

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:28.440
And you see that long bamboo
pole, and it is a depth gauge.

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:33.123
There are two of them, usually
at the prow of each boat.

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:41.520
And when they are in shallow waters,

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:45.250
there are two people
shouting out the depth

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:48.203
of the river from both
sides of the boat, actually.

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:52.980
And they're yelling more than five feet

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:55.743
or less than four feet,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:59.930
And this is exactly kind
of Mark Twain's point

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:03.080
on life of the Mississippi
marking on the twine,

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:07.110
is the equivalent of the
Mississippi river boats

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:10.140
that had to do precisely the same thing.

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:14.660
And still, it was very common
for (indistinct) to get stuck.

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:17.200
You may wonder what these
people are doing in the water.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.853
First of all, notice how
shallow it is, right?

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:25.730
This passenger boat has become stuck,

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:29.270
and what they've done is to
send two or three members

00:20:29.270 --> 00:20:33.620
of the crew out, and they have
a stake and other close up

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:36.840
they have a stake, which they embed

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:40.050
in the sand of the river.

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:44.600
They will then attach that to a cable

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:46.970
which will then be attached to a winch

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:51.540
at the back of the boat
that will winch it in

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:55.720
and winch it out and the idea is to wiggle

00:20:55.720 --> 00:21:00.587
the prow of the boat until
it can be backed off the bank

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:03.010
in which it's embedded.

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:05.280
The engines are of course going in reverse

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:10.880
but the winching is necessary
in order to unembed it.

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:15.880
And that's why actually
the ships going downstream

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:19.180
and lower have the right of way,

00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:22.280
because if you get stuck going downstream

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:25.640
the current is your enemy
because it embeds you deeper

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:27.680
and it's harder to back off.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:30.110
Whereas the ships going upstream

00:21:31.570 --> 00:21:32.620
don't have the right of way,

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:36.193
because it's much easier
for them to get off.

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:47.550
The variation over time...

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:50.453
One more thing about
rivers as living things,

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:56.850
it helps to tell us that ideas

00:21:56.850 --> 00:22:00.520
such as the a hundred year
flood or 50-year flood

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:03.010
that you hear all the
time in weather reports

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:06.750
and disaster reports is,

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:09.633
and I choose my words carefully nonsense.

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:16.100
That is to say, for example, the Rhine,

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:20.470
had four 100-year floods in 12 years

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:25.010
in 1983, '88, '93 and '94.

00:22:25.010 --> 00:22:26.680
That should give one some pause

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:29.290
over the meaning of a hundred year flood.

00:22:29.290 --> 00:22:30.542
Why?

00:22:30.542 --> 00:22:33.820
For most rivers, not quite all of them

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:36.600
we simply don't have
the statistical series

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:39.310
on water levels and volume flows

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:42.020
that go back more than
a hundred, 200 years,

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:43.570
which would even in principle

00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:47.710
allow us to make such a
statistical generalization.

00:22:47.710 --> 00:22:50.060
Now the Yellow River or
the Yangtze or the Rhine

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:53.410
or Danube or Euphrates are
partial exceptions to this,

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:56.520
and we have a longer statistical series.

00:22:56.520 --> 00:23:00.820
But more important, even if
we did have a deep series,

00:23:00.820 --> 00:23:03.461
the assumption behind
the a hundred year flood

00:23:03.461 --> 00:23:05.040
and the 50-year flood

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:08.533
is the assumption of
hydrological equilibrium,

00:23:10.130 --> 00:23:13.393
but it's not the same
river from year to year.

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:18.910
It's moving silt and sand and clay,

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:21.870
it's carving new channels and meanders,

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:25.900
it's normal flood stage is
building natural levees.

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:30.500
And if we add anthropogenic
changes in particular

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:33.660
it's radically not the same river.

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:38.200
That is to say in 1800 most
of the Mississippi bank

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:41.650
was forested, but by 1960,

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:46.390
95% was planted to agricultural crops.

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:48.700
Forget about all the other interventions

00:23:48.700 --> 00:23:50.463
which are made into the river.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:56.870
My problem in my seminar with the students

00:23:56.870 --> 00:24:01.860
on rivers was that
they're mostly interested

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:05.350
in actually like the Philippine
hydrologist I mentioned,

00:24:05.350 --> 00:24:07.900
they're interested in
the uses of the river,

00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:10.690
for homo sapiens, for drinking water

00:24:10.690 --> 00:24:15.690
for irrigation, et cetera, flood control.

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:21.860
And I hope when I write the
book about the Irrawaddy,

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:26.373
because I have a lot of data
on fish migrations and so on

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:32.770
and migrations of birds and
riverine mammals as well,

00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:35.330
to talk more about all those creatures

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:39.200
that depend on the river
for their life world,

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:44.077
including clams and mussels,
shellfish, shell and larvae.

00:24:47.570 --> 00:24:51.690
The most important movement in
the annual life of the river

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:54.293
is what is called the flood pulse.

00:24:55.890 --> 00:25:00.600
It's a very, I think
important choice of terms

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.280
that is to say that part of the year

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:07.160
during which the river
overflows its natural channel

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:11.153
and occupies its habitual floodplain.

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:15.570
The pulse of high water
may come from the monsoon,

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:18.624
from snow or glacial melt
or from seasonal rainfall.

00:25:18.624 --> 00:25:21.920
And it may be of different
degrees of variability

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:24.960
but it is a completely natural part

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:27.033
of the annual cycle of a river.

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:31.620
The flooding of the floodplain
represents the lungs

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:33.810
of a river in a literal sense.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:36.580
That is to say the
condition of it's vitality

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:40.030
and its nutrition and that of
the creatures who depend on it

00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:44.960
without the annual flooding
of the floodplain, the channel

00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:47.800
which we usually associate
with the river at rest

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:51.810
in our paintings and photographs
is comparatively dead,

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:52.993
biologically speaking.

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:57.300
Flood as a scare word is
so deeply anthropogenic

00:25:57.300 --> 00:26:00.393
that I want to ban its use
but it's not in my power.

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:04.533
It's just the river breathing as it must.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:09.480
On this view, we would understand
a flooding of settlements

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:13.810
near the river as the result
of homo sapiens encroaching

00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:15.750
on the natural plane of the river,

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.203
that is to say an act of trespass.

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:23.450
The periodic flooding of the
floodplain is the life world

00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:26.730
and condition of existence
of all of the species

00:26:26.730 --> 00:26:31.450
that inhabit the river or
who dwell along the river.

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:36.450
And here, this is an effort
by a disturbance ecologist

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:45.110
to show you how the level
of a river over time

00:26:46.895 --> 00:26:50.583
brings nutrients to
higher and higher ground,

00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:53.883
and then retreats.

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:58.976
Fish, for example, get as much as 80%

00:26:58.976 --> 00:27:01.240
of their total annual nutrition

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:05.300
from the huge pulse of food
that the flood stage affords,

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:07.513
as they spread out over the floodplain.

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:11.140
There, they spawn and put on weight,

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:15.270
they feed on the invertebrates,
decaying organic matter,

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:18.080
the microbes that are on the floodplain.

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:20.720
There's almost always a
huge migration of fish

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:22.810
to take advantage of this feeding frenzy

00:27:24.547 --> 00:27:28.450
anadromous fish that come in
from the sea for that matter,

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:33.253
are there in partly in large
part for this sea pulse event.

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:38.900
The floodplain may be 40
times wider than the channel

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:44.940
in places like the Amazon,
where there's a huge variation.

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:48.200
You have fruit eating fish
for the simple reason,

00:27:48.200 --> 00:27:53.200
that the water level gets so
high, that the fish can profit

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:56.550
from what we call the low hanging fruits

00:27:57.440 --> 00:28:00.833
that are on the adjacent banks.

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:07.690
The Mississippi fish catch had declined

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:12.690
more than 80% in the years up to 1993.

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:16.500
But the result of the 1993 massive flood

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:19.770
was that the fish catch
the year afterwards

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:23.340
was a new record and the year
after that also a new record

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:28.230
because of the spawning and
greater population of fish.

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:30.400
The studies of the Danube
have shown that the greater

00:28:30.400 --> 00:28:32.800
the extent of the flooded a given year,

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:35.600
the greater the haul of fish.

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:37.860
By the way, it's not just fish of course,

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:40.400
but a whole cavalcade
of all those creatures

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:43.973
that depend on a
concentration of nutrition.

00:28:44.870 --> 00:28:48.240
Waterfowl who were there
because the birds are there,

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:53.240
and the (indistinct), the way
in which fishermen can tell

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:56.000
that there's a migration of fish coming,

00:28:56.000 --> 00:29:00.510
is that the birds appear
before the fish migrate

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:03.760
because they've in a sense
learned further downstream

00:29:03.760 --> 00:29:05.950
that the fish are on the way

00:29:05.950 --> 00:29:10.393
and they precede the fish
and wait for their feast.

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:14.620
And the fishermen then know
it's time to get out of the nets

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:17.823
and get ready for the migration of fish.

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:24.780
Waterfowl and riverine wetland
birds in the West Heron,

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:28.203
muskrats, fox, wolves Raptors herbivores,

00:29:29.115 --> 00:29:32.000
will come for the fresh grouse, excuse me,

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:35.210
fresh grass sprouting
after the flood recedes,

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:38.723
and all the microparasites
that feed on this cavalcade.

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:43.710
And then as the flood recedes,
much of this nutrition

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:48.320
returns to the channel where
some of the larger fish,

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:51.750
channel cats and dolphins
can benefit from it,

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:55.380
as well as the comparatively
immobile, shellfish, clams

00:29:55.380 --> 00:29:56.530
and bottom larvae.

00:29:56.530 --> 00:30:00.450
That is to say the flood pulse
brings back to the channel

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:04.020
nutrition that it would not otherwise have

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:07.850
that it collects if you
like from the floodplain.

00:30:07.850 --> 00:30:09.300
So what the flood does

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:12.150
in one sense is to provide connectivity.

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:14.010
It moves water over the landscape

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:18.140
creating a huge variety of
habitats, backwaters, ponds

00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:22.913
marsh environments, moving
water, a warmer water, refugees

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:26.340
or refuges from larger predators,

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:29.140
varied assemblages of food and habitat

00:30:29.140 --> 00:30:32.240
that favor different riverine species.

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:36.380
The whole mechanism depends
on the microbial richness

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:39.190
of the floodlight that represents the base

00:30:39.190 --> 00:30:40.950
of the food pyramid

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:43.933
and the entire life world of the river.

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:47.120
Without the flood pulse then,

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:48.893
the river is comparatively dead.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:53.700
I mean to emphasize non-human species here

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:54.640
but let me say a bit

00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:58.403
about what the flood pulse
does for homo sapiens,

00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:03.610
the most numerous and most
successful invasive species

00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:04.763
the world has known.

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:10.240
Well, what it does is civilization,

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:13.260
no floodplain, no civilization.

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:15.220
That's something that
was slight exaggeration

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:18.497
but not a great exaggeration exception.

00:31:19.352 --> 00:31:21.780
(indistinct) or archaic civilizations

00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:24.120
were founded on floodplains,

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:26.930
often near the estuary of a river.

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:28.410
Why?

00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:31.300
That's the only place where
you can have a concentration

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:36.220
of foodstuffs and people
in a small circumference

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:38.233
in which state making is possible.

00:31:40.690 --> 00:31:42.960
Observe what the flood does.

00:31:42.960 --> 00:31:47.393
It drowns all the competing
vegetation, AKA weeds,

00:31:48.710 --> 00:31:52.570
it lays down a layer of nutritious silt

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:55.363
that provides nutrients to the crops.

00:31:56.350 --> 00:31:57.783
Usually cereal grains.

00:31:58.730 --> 00:32:00.650
It provides if it's well behaved

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:05.080
as the Nile Valley generally
is a perfectly harrowed field

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:07.800
ready for sowing, no plowing needed,

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:10.340
just broadcast the seeds.

00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:14.610
In a sense, the flood
does the agricultural work

00:32:14.610 --> 00:32:17.350
except for the sowing and the harvesting.

00:32:17.350 --> 00:32:22.270
And the same way, by the way,
if you, the comparable case

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:27.270
for inland agriculture
in the hills is shifting

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:31.040
or fire field cultivation
or/and burn cultivation

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:35.300
in which the job of that water does

00:32:35.300 --> 00:32:39.323
in what I call flood
recession agriculture,

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:43.970
the job that fire does is
exactly what water does

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:45.650
in flood recession agriculture.

00:32:45.650 --> 00:32:50.650
That is to say it burns organic matter,

00:32:51.230 --> 00:32:55.810
provides fertilizer and
all the other fields

00:32:55.810 --> 00:32:58.610
have to be moved from time to time.

00:32:58.610 --> 00:33:01.133
It's essentially the same process.

00:33:05.232 --> 00:33:07.777
Hence the oldest form of agriculture

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:11.680
is called flood recession agriculture

00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:13.757
or in French (speaks in foreign language).

00:33:17.602 --> 00:33:22.602
It's also common in Eastern
Woodland agriculture

00:33:23.240 --> 00:33:24.913
among native Americans.

00:33:25.850 --> 00:33:28.810
These would be the places along streams

00:33:28.810 --> 00:33:31.960
that were annually flooded,
that would be the places

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:36.493
where native Americans
would do early planting.

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:44.387
Make sure I've got them, there we go.

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:51.950
This is an island in the upper Irrawaddy

00:33:55.520 --> 00:34:00.520
and it is completely flood
recession agriculture.

00:34:02.020 --> 00:34:07.020
And it is something like five or 600 acres

00:34:13.090 --> 00:34:15.810
that is recreated every year

00:34:15.810 --> 00:34:20.810
and that six villages share
or fight about, I should say

00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:22.550
in terms of who gets what

00:34:22.550 --> 00:34:26.290
because this patch is changing every year

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:30.460
depending on the
accumulation of silt and soil

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:33.680
but it's a major agricultural site

00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:38.570
and there is bamboo bridge
rebuild nearly two miles long

00:34:38.570 --> 00:34:41.550
built every year in
order that people can get

00:34:41.550 --> 00:34:46.550
from one bank to this
island in the middle.

00:34:46.640 --> 00:34:51.640
And in the Delta and the Irrawaddy
Delta, there are hundreds

00:34:53.060 --> 00:34:58.060
and hundreds of such islands
with complicated forms

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:02.200
of property rights with efforts to shore

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:06.050
up property rights by
driving in bamboo stakes

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:10.080
and making sure that the new
silt accumulates on their side

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:13.610
of the island rather than
on other side of the island.

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:16.890
So it's a whole world in which our sense

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:21.890
of if you like land staying
in place, doesn't hold,

00:35:23.020 --> 00:35:27.480
and it is if you like shifting floodplains

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:30.110
that are in the middle of a river

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:33.060
or off shore from its banks

00:35:37.180 --> 00:35:38.910
The best way of understanding this,

00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:43.193
is through what is often
called disturbance ecology.

00:35:45.150 --> 00:35:46.970
I don't like the term disturbance

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:50.110
because it risks obscuring the fact

00:35:50.110 --> 00:35:54.070
that floods are often annual,
natural, unfairly predictable.

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:57.260
That is to say, they're
not disturbances at all

00:35:57.260 --> 00:36:02.247
or such disturbances are normal
cyclical, but it is proposed

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:08.100
in contrast to distinction,
to equilibrium analysis,

00:36:08.100 --> 00:36:10.080
in which the succession and distribution,

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:14.090
efficient insects and birds
reached a terminal state

00:36:14.090 --> 00:36:15.193
that was stable.

00:36:16.100 --> 00:36:19.650
As we shall see, it's the prevention

00:36:20.810 --> 00:36:24.900
or the forceful stopping of
these natural perturbations,

00:36:24.900 --> 00:36:29.180
that is the real disturbance,
the real intervention

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:33.363
the attempt to enforce
a permanent equilibrium.

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:37.390
Natural disturbances create new mosaics

00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:39.560
with plants and animals, insects.

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:43.560
They open the canopy, they
destroy or displace species

00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:46.543
but they begin a new
succession of colonists.

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:50.200
The result is more biodiversity

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:52.770
in terms of the variety of species

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:57.770
and this mosaic of patches
that is in turn more resilient.

00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:02.140
It's worth recalling what
the flood accomplishes,

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:04.660
it eliminates much previous vegetation,

00:37:04.660 --> 00:37:09.660
it's soaks the soil deeply,
it brings in nutritious silt,

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:14.440
and it is an open and inviting environment

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:16.760
waiting to be colonized

00:37:16.760 --> 00:37:21.760
by flood adapted marine life,
by flood adapted insect life

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:25.400
by flood adaptive flora
seeds that have been waiting

00:37:25.400 --> 00:37:27.360
for a long, long time.

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:30.623
Quick colonizing species
from adjacent patches,

00:37:31.570 --> 00:37:34.490
a slower colonization from further away,

00:37:34.490 --> 00:37:36.610
it's a favorable spawning environment

00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:41.090
for a selection of fish, insects and birds

00:37:41.090 --> 00:37:44.970
who do best in uncrowded
open environments.

00:37:44.970 --> 00:37:48.830
It creates then innumerable
edge environments

00:37:48.830 --> 00:37:51.090
or ecotones that are, as we know,

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:54.550
species rich because they provide access

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:57.653
to two or more environments,
sometimes seasonal.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:00.663
This is what happens without humans,

00:38:02.590 --> 00:38:05.647
humans can use and shape this process.

00:38:05.647 --> 00:38:07.790
So along the Nile, just poking a hole

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:12.330
in the natural levees of
the river at a flood stage

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:14.630
could inundate the back slopes and hollows

00:38:14.630 --> 00:38:16.550
with silt rich water.

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:18.800
And then when the water had soaked in,

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:23.800
one could plant wheat or
barley, no state was needed.

00:38:24.050 --> 00:38:29.050
So if you like the agriculture
along the Nile River

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:32.120
was a completely village
affair had nothing to do

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:37.120
with Pharaohs and state-like
entities creating irrigation.

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:41.210
Flood recession agriculture is simplest,

00:38:41.210 --> 00:38:45.563
it just uses nature to
work with a little nudge.

00:38:48.760 --> 00:38:53.760
Now, how am I doing in terms
of time, Eric, five minutes?

00:38:55.140 --> 00:38:57.460
- You have a little bit
more than five minutes.

00:38:57.460 --> 00:39:01.570
- I'm sorry, I've got to put on my, yeah.

00:39:01.570 --> 00:39:03.183
- You have five minutes.

00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:09.020
- Okay, good, what we've done with rivers

00:39:09.540 --> 00:39:12.933
is to simplify them by engineering.

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:16.840
Humankind has of course
been sculpting rivers

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:18.373
for its purposes forever.

00:39:19.330 --> 00:39:21.820
But only with the invention of dynamite

00:39:21.820 --> 00:39:26.220
in 1870 and subsequent
earth moving machinery

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:28.590
and reinforced concrete

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:32.463
as this sculpting taken
on protean proportions.

00:39:33.300 --> 00:39:35.070
As you know, one of the interesting things

00:39:35.070 --> 00:39:36.170
about the Irrawaddy,

00:39:36.170 --> 00:39:39.360
is that the Irrawaddy
does not have any dams

00:39:39.360 --> 00:39:41.330
on its main stem.

00:39:41.330 --> 00:39:44.770
The effort to build a dam at the Myitsone

00:39:46.240 --> 00:39:49.900
at the origin of the Irrawaddy,
which was underway actually

00:39:49.900 --> 00:39:54.880
and was stopped by actually
a democratic movement

00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:58.940
and general's compliance with
that democratic movement.

00:39:58.940 --> 00:40:03.940
There are lots of dams north
of the origin of the Irrawaddy

00:40:04.070 --> 00:40:06.820
on the tributaries that
contribute to the Irrawaddy

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:09.620
and they've had a huge
effect on the hydrology

00:40:09.620 --> 00:40:12.060
of the Irrawaddy, but the Irrawaddy itself

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:16.423
is so far an undammed river.

00:40:21.880 --> 00:40:26.540
The engineering of rivers
is I like to think of it

00:40:26.540 --> 00:40:30.410
as some combination of
taxidermy and amputation

00:40:34.530 --> 00:40:38.400
because the simplification of the effort

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:42.020
of these intervention is
usually to simplify the river

00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:45.223
and to have it serve a
single functional purpose.

00:40:46.070 --> 00:40:48.833
The Rhine is a striking example,

00:40:49.861 --> 00:40:52.340
Johann Johann Gottfried Tulla around 1800,

00:40:53.550 --> 00:40:56.310
responsible for a treaty called

00:40:56.310 --> 00:40:58.050
the Rectification of the Rhine

00:40:58.050 --> 00:41:02.530
among all the adjacent Rhine nations.

00:41:02.530 --> 00:41:04.950
The wonderful word
Rectification of the Rhine

00:41:05.980 --> 00:41:08.906
is a wonderful term as if God had somehow,

00:41:08.906 --> 00:41:10.250
if you'll excuse the expression fucked up

00:41:10.250 --> 00:41:12.120
and that the Rhine had to be rectified

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:15.353
from its original form.

00:41:18.868 --> 00:41:21.340
It was an actual treaty,
it eliminated meanders,

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:23.093
removed barriers in the channel,

00:41:24.310 --> 00:41:25.620
it confined the river to a single bed

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:30.010
with no braids to a uniform
width where possible

00:41:30.010 --> 00:41:33.800
through a uniform depth and
a uniform speed of current.

00:41:33.800 --> 00:41:36.680
That is to say the effort
was to turn the Rhine

00:41:36.680 --> 00:41:41.680
into a canal so far as it
was possible, predictable,

00:41:41.740 --> 00:41:43.810
uniform, straight,

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:47.193
a Burnett Willy's principle
of pressure and turbulence,

00:41:48.270 --> 00:41:51.410
to take a variable and
turn it into a constant.

00:41:51.410 --> 00:41:55.290
The Rhine was shortened by 105 kilometers

00:41:56.420 --> 00:42:01.420
and it lost 80% of its
floodplain in the upper Rhine.

00:42:07.200 --> 00:42:12.200
I'm going to skip some things.

00:42:15.180 --> 00:42:18.207
I should add, there's the Rhine.

00:42:27.410 --> 00:42:29.140
And you can see the old Rhine,

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:34.140
and the new reformed Rhine as
it was shortened over time.

00:42:38.930 --> 00:42:42.010
That section that I
skipped over by the way

00:42:42.010 --> 00:42:43.560
is a section on the way

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:48.560
in which people running away
from States have run to marshes

00:42:50.740 --> 00:42:55.480
and wetlands because they're
extremely hard to please,

00:42:55.480 --> 00:42:57.200
whether it's the Marsh Arabs,

00:42:57.200 --> 00:42:59.110
whether it's the Great Dismal Swamp

00:42:59.110 --> 00:43:02.650
that held more than
6,000 and runaway slaves

00:43:02.650 --> 00:43:06.630
at the beginning of the
Civil War, marshes and swamps

00:43:06.630 --> 00:43:11.630
have been in, if you
like refuges over time

00:43:13.172 --> 00:43:17.220
for people wanting to evade the state

00:43:17.220 --> 00:43:19.083
for one reason or another.

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:25.290
The result of the
Rectification of the Rhine

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:28.710
is that a drop of water that
falls in Switzerland now

00:43:29.810 --> 00:43:33.410
takes only three days to get to Holland,

00:43:33.410 --> 00:43:37.320
whereas before it took
something like 10 days.

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:40.010
The results of this
for catastrophic floods

00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:43.370
in a high water are something
that you can appreciate

00:43:43.370 --> 00:43:47.580
and the degree to which a big
rainfall is quickly channeled

00:43:47.580 --> 00:43:52.040
into the river because of
the changes of its banks

00:43:52.040 --> 00:43:54.030
and the changes in the channel.

00:43:54.030 --> 00:43:55.850
George Perkins Marsh understood this

00:43:55.850 --> 00:43:57.960
as a simple matter of physics,

00:43:57.960 --> 00:44:02.960
say a river has a 1600 mile
course, say the natural channel

00:44:04.060 --> 00:44:07.800
drops 800 feet from the origin to the sea,

00:44:07.800 --> 00:44:10.163
that's a drop of six inches to the mile.

00:44:12.030 --> 00:44:14.510
By shortening the river
and cutting off meanders,

00:44:14.510 --> 00:44:18.520
let's say you make it
only 1200 miles long.

00:44:18.520 --> 00:44:22.263
Then the pitch is increased
eight inches to the mile,

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:27.717
it's only two inches,
it's one quarter steeper

00:44:30.980 --> 00:44:33.888
but it has tremendous effects on the speed

00:44:33.888 --> 00:44:35.910
on how much silt it carries on the height

00:44:35.910 --> 00:44:39.803
of probable flood crests
on the erosion of banks.

00:44:40.750 --> 00:44:43.160
And if you then try to minimize floods

00:44:43.160 --> 00:44:47.760
by preventing lateral movement
of river water, by levees

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:51.270
you increase the amount of
silt carried to the mouth,

00:44:51.270 --> 00:44:55.010
the silting up of the Dutch
estuary is a good example.

00:44:55.010 --> 00:44:59.590
As Aldo Leopold, once wrote,
"The Germans had a fondness

00:44:59.590 --> 00:45:02.117
for unnecessary outdoor geometry."

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:08.310
Other simplifications are
hydroelectric dams and irrigation

00:45:08.310 --> 00:45:11.390
which create changed lakes, still water,

00:45:11.390 --> 00:45:14.420
which destroys fish
migrations in both directions

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:18.560
and prevents the natural
conveyor belt of silt downriver

00:45:18.560 --> 00:45:22.090
even for national (indistinct) of a river.

00:45:22.090 --> 00:45:27.090
It raises the question of when
is the river still a river?

00:45:29.030 --> 00:45:31.080
How many things can you do to a river

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:33.370
and still call it a river?

00:45:33.370 --> 00:45:38.370
I once had a colleague in
Wisconsin who had a cat,

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:43.620
and since I was in Wisconsin
for the better part of a decade

00:45:45.200 --> 00:45:48.710
I would visit them, this family
often, I will not name them.

00:45:48.710 --> 00:45:52.620
And the cat, somehow the
its tail had been docked

00:45:52.620 --> 00:45:56.020
for some reason, it had been neutered

00:45:57.200 --> 00:45:59.430
and shortly in the year, before I left

00:45:59.430 --> 00:46:03.460
as it was tearing up the
furniture, it had been declawed.

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:08.460
And my wife and I on the
way back from this visit,

00:46:09.760 --> 00:46:13.040
wondered to one another how
many things could you take away

00:46:13.040 --> 00:46:15.560
from a cat and still have a cat?

00:46:15.560 --> 00:46:19.710
And somehow the decline of a cat seemed

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:24.410
somehow to be over the red line.

00:46:24.410 --> 00:46:27.270
But I think it's worth saying what happens

00:46:27.270 --> 00:46:29.660
in the engineering of river

00:46:30.750 --> 00:46:33.210
which has never completely
successful, by the way,

00:46:33.210 --> 00:46:37.513
when you try to turn it
into a canal, a pipe,

00:46:40.869 --> 00:46:44.303
a chain of lakes that can be
used for irrigation and so on.

00:46:49.810 --> 00:46:54.810
One of the results of this
effort to control a river,

00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:01.733
this is the Yellow River near Kaifeng.

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:06.200
And because of the use
of levees over time,

00:47:06.200 --> 00:47:08.490
it's a rich agricultural area,

00:47:08.490 --> 00:47:10.380
and as the river carry more silt

00:47:10.380 --> 00:47:13.750
and therefore the bed of the
river got higher and higher

00:47:13.750 --> 00:47:17.730
and higher in order to
prevent catastrophic floods

00:47:17.730 --> 00:47:20.120
the levees were built
higher and higher and higher

00:47:20.120 --> 00:47:21.170
over time.

00:47:21.170 --> 00:47:26.170
So that now at Kaifeng, at
least when this book was written

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:28.790
from which the image is taken

00:47:28.790 --> 00:47:33.790
the river was 33 feet above
the surrounding floodplain.

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:39.530
So you may wanna call that a river,

00:47:39.530 --> 00:47:43.120
but to me, it looks a
lot more like an aqueduct

00:47:43.120 --> 00:47:45.540
and it's an aqueduct that's been made

00:47:45.540 --> 00:47:47.590
by the engineering of the river

00:47:47.590 --> 00:47:50.623
that gradually increased its bed

00:47:54.002 --> 00:47:56.567
the height of its bed and
necessitated, if you wanted

00:47:58.490 --> 00:48:03.490
to protect the farmland around
it, or tax paying farmers

00:48:03.500 --> 00:48:06.430
and so on then you built the levees

00:48:06.430 --> 00:48:08.020
higher and higher and higher,

00:48:08.020 --> 00:48:12.203
and it set the stage
for catastrophic floods.

00:48:13.060 --> 00:48:16.240
So when a flood then does occur,

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:19.490
it's likely to be a
massive and enormous flood.

00:48:19.490 --> 00:48:21.100
And I want to wind up here

00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:26.100
and this requires me to vastly shorten

00:48:26.589 --> 00:48:29.530
the last part of the talk,

00:48:29.530 --> 00:48:32.150
but I'll try to summarize it briefly.

00:48:32.150 --> 00:48:36.500
There's something in medicine
called iatrogenic illnesses

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:38.610
which are a fancy term

00:48:38.610 --> 00:48:40.793
for the cure being worse than the disease.

00:48:41.910 --> 00:48:45.090
It's the superbugs that
one finds in hospitals,

00:48:45.090 --> 00:48:48.390
that in a sense by trying
to stop small infections,

00:48:48.390 --> 00:48:51.680
with antibiotics, you create
the selection pressures

00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:53.480
that increase over time

00:48:53.480 --> 00:48:58.480
the bacteria that are able
to survive these insults

00:48:58.570 --> 00:49:03.570
and poisons and still survive
and then you create superbugs

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:07.050
that thrive in hospitals and so on.

00:49:07.050 --> 00:49:09.930
I won't go into this,
but iatrogenic medicine

00:49:09.930 --> 00:49:11.080
is extremely important

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:14.060
and a tremendous number of the
hospitalizations that occur

00:49:14.060 --> 00:49:16.850
are in a sense the results of treatment

00:49:18.310 --> 00:49:22.530
rather than the illness itself.

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:27.530
So I want to argue that the effort

00:49:30.110 --> 00:49:33.990
to prevent small infections
results in large infections,

00:49:33.990 --> 00:49:37.220
the results to prevent small floods,

00:49:37.220 --> 00:49:38.670
and there you have political lock-in

00:49:38.670 --> 00:49:42.920
because representatives want
to preserve the population

00:49:42.920 --> 00:49:45.430
because they're paying taxes
because they vote for them

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:47.170
and they'll do anything they can

00:49:47.170 --> 00:49:50.260
to preserve the real estate values.

00:49:50.260 --> 00:49:54.540
You get this political lock-in as well,

00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:56.283
and the result is then,

00:49:58.040 --> 00:50:01.500
and here forest fires are a good analogy.

00:50:01.500 --> 00:50:04.650
The effort for 60 years
by the forest service

00:50:04.650 --> 00:50:08.810
to prevent all fires resulted
in catastrophic fires

00:50:08.810 --> 00:50:11.610
because of the buildup
of combustible materials,

00:50:11.610 --> 00:50:14.914
and the prevention of natural fires.

00:50:14.914 --> 00:50:18.630
And then in the same way
there's an iatrogenic process

00:50:18.630 --> 00:50:21.870
in which the effort to
prevent small floods

00:50:23.240 --> 00:50:26.070
has resulted in large floods.

00:50:26.070 --> 00:50:30.350
The 1993, a flood of the
Mississippi and Missouri

00:50:30.350 --> 00:50:31.380
is a good example.

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:36.170
And now the most, the
smallest, most hesitant

00:50:37.590 --> 00:50:42.590
and timid efforts are being
made to restore floodplains

00:50:44.950 --> 00:50:47.063
and wetlands that have been destroyed,

00:50:48.170 --> 00:50:52.960
but to an extent that
is so trivial and timid

00:50:53.940 --> 00:50:58.840
that it has not made
much of a dent in the,

00:50:58.840 --> 00:51:02.490
if you like the death of
rivers or the maiming of rivers

00:51:02.490 --> 00:51:06.720
or the taxidermy of rivers
that we experienced.

00:51:06.720 --> 00:51:09.493
Let me stop there, happy
to take any questions.

