00:52:50.810 --> 00:52:52.713
the answer is you're right.

00:52:54.210 --> 00:52:58.210
If you're interested in
the life world of the river

00:52:58.210 --> 00:53:02.590
and the migrating fish and the life forms

00:53:02.590 --> 00:53:05.910
that depend on the natural
processes of the river

00:53:07.100 --> 00:53:11.040
and of its floods and
so on, then for them,

00:53:11.040 --> 00:53:12.813
it's almost certain death.

00:53:14.450 --> 00:53:17.430
And so it depends
whether that's the point,

00:53:17.430 --> 00:53:19.350
it depends on whether you're focusing,

00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:22.410
I suppose wind and solar are rather better

00:53:22.410 --> 00:53:23.450
than hydroelectric.

00:53:23.450 --> 00:53:28.320
And there are, by the
way, small dams, a run

00:53:28.320 --> 00:53:29.620
of the river dams.

00:53:29.620 --> 00:53:33.420
I mean, there's a typology of dams

00:53:36.046 --> 00:53:41.046
which are more or less destructive as dams

00:53:41.220 --> 00:53:44.230
in terms of the natural
processes of the river.

00:53:44.230 --> 00:53:46.400
So the world commission on dams

00:53:46.400 --> 00:53:50.380
actually has a set of
principles that it established.

00:53:50.380 --> 00:53:53.460
This was after the normal dam protests

00:53:55.120 --> 00:54:00.120
for dams that are well okay,
if not entirely approved

00:54:08.370 --> 00:54:10.307
- Thanks Jim, thanks
Paul for that question,

00:54:10.307 --> 00:54:11.363
and Michael.

00:54:13.480 --> 00:54:14.513
- Thanks, Eric.

00:54:15.730 --> 00:54:17.100
This was fascinating, Jim.

00:54:17.100 --> 00:54:21.740
I was interested in your
likening of the impact

00:54:21.740 --> 00:54:26.740
of flooding to the impacts
of fire in the hills.

00:54:27.070 --> 00:54:31.210
So this gives us two pulses to compare,

00:54:31.210 --> 00:54:34.910
a pulse from wet to dry to wet

00:54:34.910 --> 00:54:39.210
and in the hills a pulse
from field to forest,

00:54:39.210 --> 00:54:41.883
back to field so two different pulses.

00:54:43.020 --> 00:54:48.020
And it seems like these
pulses have been problematic

00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:53.670
for land tenure,

00:54:53.670 --> 00:54:58.170
at least the way outsiders
viewed land tenure.

00:54:58.170 --> 00:55:02.310
So I'm wondering if you can place

00:55:02.310 --> 00:55:05.930
the tenure real
implications of these pulses

00:55:07.040 --> 00:55:12.040
in the longer history of
land tenure for fixed land

00:55:15.900 --> 00:55:20.100
within which these seem to
be, as I said, problematic

00:55:24.560 --> 00:55:25.603
- I'm thinking.

00:55:34.600 --> 00:55:38.280
I suppose, maybe this
is what you're implying

00:55:39.290 --> 00:55:42.890
in your comments, that I was interested

00:55:42.890 --> 00:55:47.057
in the nutrient cycle of silt versus fire

00:55:48.100 --> 00:55:53.100
and the way in which it
provided the basis for cropping.

00:55:53.480 --> 00:55:57.050
But you raise the question,
which is even more interesting

00:55:57.050 --> 00:56:02.050
in many ways, which is
our idea of property

00:56:03.580 --> 00:56:08.580
that is based on fixed borders
and fixed tenure and titles

00:56:13.070 --> 00:56:18.070
when in fact, in the flood
recession agriculture

00:56:18.760 --> 00:56:22.060
that I'm talking about the
land is shifting all the time

00:56:22.970 --> 00:56:25.027
under the pressure of floods

00:56:25.027 --> 00:56:28.740
and the deposition of
silted sand and gravel

00:56:31.579 --> 00:56:36.412
for/and burn agriculture,
shifting agriculture.

00:56:38.070 --> 00:56:43.040
It requires that in a sense,
the fields to be moved

00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:48.040
but it's within as you know
a lot better than I do,

00:56:49.450 --> 00:56:54.450
it's within a fixed compass by and large

00:56:54.480 --> 00:56:57.947
or if you like a rotation of fallowing.

00:56:59.662 --> 00:57:02.329
And as again, what's interesting

00:57:05.460 --> 00:57:07.880
is in terms of customary land tenure,

00:57:07.880 --> 00:57:11.460
the people who are not
planning particular Sweden

00:57:11.460 --> 00:57:14.940
are likely to have recognized rights

00:57:14.940 --> 00:57:19.940
to the things that are popping
up in the Sweden anyway,

00:57:19.970 --> 00:57:22.370
and the right to return
to her, is that correct?

00:57:25.590 --> 00:57:26.423
- [Michael] Yes.

00:57:28.970 --> 00:57:31.070
- I'm not sure I answered
your question, Michael,

00:57:31.070 --> 00:57:32.550
I wouldn't like to hear a lecture

00:57:32.550 --> 00:57:33.900
from you on the comparison.

00:57:36.240 --> 00:57:37.740
- That was great, thanks, Jim.

00:57:38.600 --> 00:57:41.520
- I think we can have a
special workshop on this topic

00:57:41.520 --> 00:57:44.370
with Michael and Jim
meeting, head to head.

00:57:44.370 --> 00:57:46.053
We have a question from Jacob.

00:57:47.150 --> 00:57:50.910
- Hi, Professor Scott, that
was a really interesting,

00:57:50.910 --> 00:57:53.190
I really appreciated hearing

00:57:53.190 --> 00:57:56.220
about all the river meandering issues,

00:57:56.220 --> 00:57:57.053
but just something

00:57:57.053 --> 00:58:00.313
that's certainly been on my mind recently,

00:58:01.335 --> 00:58:04.560
I guess I'm gonna keep my
question short as requested,

00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:06.900
I'm wondering about the implications

00:58:06.900 --> 00:58:09.710
of a world of real liberated rivers

00:58:09.710 --> 00:58:11.200
where they're permitted to meander,

00:58:11.200 --> 00:58:12.470
they're permitted to be dynamic

00:58:12.470 --> 00:58:14.750
and they're permitted to
have their flood cycles.

00:58:14.750 --> 00:58:19.750
The implications for human
settlement, urbanization, town

00:58:20.140 --> 00:58:23.530
and city location patterns,
implications for transportation.

00:58:23.530 --> 00:58:25.410
I was struck by your example

00:58:25.410 --> 00:58:27.530
of the temporary seasonal bamboo bridge,

00:58:27.530 --> 00:58:32.530
I've seen bridges like that
in (indistinct) in India

00:58:32.660 --> 00:58:35.120
at a particular river location,
where during some times

00:58:35.120 --> 00:58:37.670
at a year, you get across on a ferry boat,

00:58:37.670 --> 00:58:39.960
and another times of year,
you have to use an elephant

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:41.890
to wade across the elephant swim

00:58:41.890 --> 00:58:44.970
in the deep part of the channel
with people on its back.

00:58:44.970 --> 00:58:46.680
And then at another time of
year, when it's appropriate

00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:48.650
you have this temporary bridge.

00:58:48.650 --> 00:58:51.320
And that's really neat in
that particular location,

00:58:51.320 --> 00:58:52.910
it's a village called (indistinct).

00:58:52.910 --> 00:58:56.370
But people were talking about reliberating

00:58:56.370 --> 00:58:59.963
the entire lower Rhine or
the entire lower Mississippi.

00:59:00.900 --> 00:59:04.700
What happens to town
in a city settlements?

00:59:04.700 --> 00:59:06.050
What happens to road infrastructure?

00:59:06.050 --> 00:59:08.150
How do people get from point A to point B,

00:59:10.030 --> 00:59:13.410
even if rivers are constantly meandering

00:59:13.410 --> 00:59:14.770
and that means that sandbars

00:59:14.770 --> 00:59:16.250
are constantly being rearranged.

00:59:16.250 --> 00:59:20.070
So even things like having
regular ferry routes

00:59:20.070 --> 00:59:20.940
might be difficult.

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:24.520
So I'm wondering how could
you kind of reintegrate

00:59:24.520 --> 00:59:25.820
those kinds of human needs

00:59:25.820 --> 00:59:27.913
with these with reliberated rivers?

00:59:30.120 --> 00:59:35.120
- It's a great question
and my job is to diagnose

00:59:35.630 --> 00:59:37.783
where we are not how we get out of it.

00:59:39.770 --> 00:59:44.770
And so what's interesting
to me for example,

00:59:46.610 --> 00:59:51.610
is that this is actually
covered kind of brilliantly

00:59:52.350 --> 00:59:55.070
and Mark Elvin's "The
Retreat of the Elephants",

00:59:55.070 --> 00:59:56.570
since you mentioned elephants.

00:59:57.430 --> 01:00:02.410
The difference between,
there were traditionally

01:00:02.410 --> 01:00:07.410
in China river masters,
and there were two schools

01:00:08.350 --> 01:00:13.350
of river mastery, a Confucian
School and a Daois School.

01:00:14.860 --> 01:00:16.030
And the Confucian school

01:00:16.030 --> 01:00:19.370
was sort of like the Army Corps
of Engineers, more or less

01:00:19.370 --> 01:00:21.670
let's make the river do
what we want it to do.

01:00:23.001 --> 01:00:28.001
And the Daois School was to try to work

01:00:30.550 --> 01:00:35.550
within the limits of rivers
and natural movement.

01:00:35.810 --> 01:00:40.100
So they would have, for
example, gates or sluices

01:00:40.100 --> 01:00:42.760
that would be over spills sluices,

01:00:42.760 --> 01:00:45.400
that would only open when
the pressure of the water

01:00:45.400 --> 01:00:48.120
reached a certain level naturally

01:00:48.120 --> 01:00:50.000
without any human intervention,

01:00:50.000 --> 01:00:51.803
they were engineered that way.

01:00:52.840 --> 01:00:57.250
The other example that is in a combination

01:00:57.250 --> 01:01:00.720
I think it's above St. Louis
on the Mississippi River

01:01:00.720 --> 01:01:05.493
in which a whole series of
areas were recently developed

01:01:07.150 --> 01:01:09.970
and were destroyed something
like two or three times

01:01:09.970 --> 01:01:12.173
in a course of 20 years.

01:01:13.470 --> 01:01:16.300
And the result was actually

01:01:16.300 --> 01:01:20.500
to forbid rebuilding in those areas.

01:01:20.500 --> 01:01:23.130
First of all, it was hard
to get insurance for them

01:01:23.130 --> 01:01:26.223
as you might imagine,
the third time around.

01:01:27.430 --> 01:01:30.650
And the result was that you had this land

01:01:30.650 --> 01:01:33.750
was actually very good agricultural land

01:01:33.750 --> 01:01:38.750
and very good for sports field land.

01:01:39.890 --> 01:01:43.220
It just couldn't have
permanent structures on it,

01:01:43.220 --> 01:01:45.800
so most of the year it
was perfectly usable

01:01:46.830 --> 01:01:51.830
but it was unoccupied and
a potential floodplain

01:01:52.310 --> 01:01:56.660
in those areas when the river rose.

01:01:56.660 --> 01:01:58.970
So it was, if you like an overspill

01:01:58.970 --> 01:02:03.940
and it ended up protecting
downriver settlements

01:02:03.940 --> 01:02:07.770
that were otherwise in danger.

01:02:07.770 --> 01:02:10.430
if there were not this overspill area.

01:02:10.430 --> 01:02:15.430
There's a history by the way
of struggle between settlements

01:02:23.560 --> 01:02:26.880
on opposite sides of a
river that's in flood stage

01:02:28.220 --> 01:02:31.533
of who's going to break
the dykes on which side.

01:02:32.461 --> 01:02:36.570
And so here's a whole Japanese literature

01:02:36.570 --> 01:02:40.580
on people rolling over from
one village to break the dykes

01:02:40.580 --> 01:02:42.380
on their competing settlement

01:02:42.380 --> 01:02:44.030
on the other side of the river,

01:02:44.030 --> 01:02:46.583
so that it releases the pressure on them,

01:02:48.060 --> 01:02:52.447
and in a sense there's I think
a series of accommodation.

01:02:59.430 --> 01:03:01.990
So the problem the
reason why I don't think

01:03:01.990 --> 01:03:04.610
it has a solution in your terms

01:03:04.610 --> 01:03:08.670
is because of political
lock-in that you have

01:03:08.670 --> 01:03:13.480
if you like a whole series
of investment, population,

01:03:13.480 --> 01:03:17.430
congressional districts, industries

01:03:17.430 --> 01:03:19.750
and so on that are built on the floodplain

01:03:20.730 --> 01:03:25.730
and that can, in a sense,
mandate the public expenditure

01:03:26.610 --> 01:03:31.610
in order to protect them,
and also lobby the government

01:03:31.610 --> 01:03:35.880
for reduced insurance rates
in order to protect them

01:03:35.880 --> 01:03:40.210
with the result, of course,
that you set yourself up

01:03:40.210 --> 01:03:43.353
for larger disasters over time I think.

01:03:46.690 --> 01:03:47.900
- Thank you.

01:03:47.900 --> 01:03:50.852
- Thanks, Jim and thanks
Jacob for that question.

01:03:50.852 --> 01:03:52.190
Now we have a question

01:03:52.190 --> 01:03:55.830
from Ian Baird who's
coming in from Madison

01:03:55.830 --> 01:03:58.010
probably defending the cats of Madison

01:03:58.010 --> 01:03:59.480
as well as having a question.

01:03:59.480 --> 01:04:03.430
- Well, I won't make any
comments about the cats

01:04:03.430 --> 01:04:06.280
but I will say that that was a great talk

01:04:06.280 --> 01:04:07.760
and thank you so much for that.

01:04:07.760 --> 01:04:12.760
I just wanted to add one
aspect to what you presented,

01:04:12.870 --> 01:04:15.790
which relates to an idea of energy.

01:04:15.790 --> 01:04:18.230
And when I talk about energy,

01:04:18.230 --> 01:04:21.020
I'm not talking about
electricity, that's produced

01:04:21.020 --> 01:04:24.270
or energy that's given to human use.

01:04:24.270 --> 01:04:26.160
I'm talking about the energy of the river

01:04:26.160 --> 01:04:28.390
when it flows down stream.

01:04:28.390 --> 01:04:30.420
And one of the things
that happens in the water,

01:04:30.420 --> 01:04:32.630
and also in the Mekong where I work

01:04:32.630 --> 01:04:35.800
is when the fish migrate upstream

01:04:36.722 --> 01:04:40.400
many of them migrate upstream,
and then they spawn upstream.

01:04:40.400 --> 01:04:43.460
And then it coincides with the increasing

01:04:43.460 --> 01:04:47.120
of the monsoon rains

01:04:47.120 --> 01:04:49.290
and the increasing of
the size of the river.

01:04:49.290 --> 01:04:53.060
And the energy of the river actually helps

01:04:53.060 --> 01:04:56.260
those small larvae that are born

01:04:56.260 --> 01:04:59.164
to flow down the river.

01:04:59.164 --> 01:05:03.330
And one of the the huge
impacts of hydropower dams,

01:05:03.330 --> 01:05:07.080
big dams is actually they stop the energy.

01:05:07.080 --> 01:05:10.670
What they do is they, you
think the dams produce energy,

01:05:10.670 --> 01:05:12.180
well, they do, but they stop the energy

01:05:12.180 --> 01:05:14.760
in the sense that they
often create reservoirs.

01:05:14.760 --> 01:05:18.380
And so those fish or
larvae that are downstream

01:05:18.380 --> 01:05:21.190
all of a sudden reach the reservoir

01:05:21.190 --> 01:05:25.790
and then the speed slows
down and these larvae,

01:05:25.790 --> 01:05:29.610
kind of drop out and often
get trapped in the reservoir,

01:05:29.610 --> 01:05:30.900
and even if they could make it

01:05:30.900 --> 01:05:33.810
through the turbines further
downstream, they don't

01:05:33.810 --> 01:05:37.500
because of the disruption
of the energy flow

01:05:37.500 --> 01:05:40.470
of the river itself or the life pulse,

01:05:40.470 --> 01:05:42.470
if you might put it that way.

01:05:42.470 --> 01:05:45.851
So I think that that's just
another way of, I think,

01:05:45.851 --> 01:05:48.730
adding to what already
a great presentation

01:05:48.730 --> 01:05:53.730
of the ecological aspects and
how they're very much related.

01:05:53.960 --> 01:05:57.680
I mean, the Mekong itself has
the largest freshwater fishery

01:05:57.680 --> 01:06:02.210
in the world, and this destruction of flow

01:06:02.210 --> 01:06:04.400
and similarly with a Wadi

01:06:05.430 --> 01:06:07.930
if they would have built
a dam on the main stream

01:06:07.930 --> 01:06:11.010
or even on many tributaries
for that matter,

01:06:11.010 --> 01:06:13.080
has really a devastating impact.

01:06:13.080 --> 01:06:17.850
- Right, I wanna start by
saying, I've learned a lot

01:06:17.850 --> 01:06:20.350
about the Mekong and about rivers

01:06:20.350 --> 01:06:24.083
from reading some of
your work on the Mekong.

01:06:26.388 --> 01:06:29.283
I think it's not appreciated,

01:06:31.780 --> 01:06:35.470
we were very much aware of these days

01:06:35.470 --> 01:06:40.470
of 4,000 mile migrations by birds annually

01:06:43.080 --> 01:06:46.420
and the importance of
these flyaways and so on.

01:06:46.420 --> 01:06:51.420
And I think we don't realize
the extraordinary distances

01:06:52.180 --> 01:06:56.977
of anadromous and I
forget what the antonym is

01:07:00.240 --> 01:07:05.240
for that fish that breed in fresh water

01:07:05.390 --> 01:07:06.763
and go to salt water.

01:07:07.781 --> 01:07:08.957
- Diatomaceous?

01:07:08.957 --> 01:07:13.957
- Right, the extent of
migrations is of distances

01:07:18.240 --> 01:07:20.640
that are astounding, just as challenging

01:07:20.640 --> 01:07:25.603
as they are for birds of
four, five, six, 7,000 miles.

01:07:26.954 --> 01:07:29.240
I mean, the movement of eels, for example,

01:07:29.240 --> 01:07:31.970
in the Pacific and so on.

01:07:31.970 --> 01:07:36.970
And so it's as if you
somehow had a barrier

01:07:39.080 --> 01:07:42.101
that destroyed the flyway
for bird migrations,

01:07:42.101 --> 01:07:44.450
every time you put in a dam

01:07:44.450 --> 01:07:48.730
not only is the still
water much less nutritious

01:07:48.730 --> 01:07:53.370
and much less biotically
alive than moving water

01:07:53.370 --> 01:07:57.980
but you also prevent
the movement, nutrition

01:07:57.980 --> 01:08:01.853
and the in a sense, the life
world, this is their medium,

01:08:04.259 --> 01:08:06.840
for them, the river is their life world.

01:08:06.840 --> 01:08:11.417
That's why we may not wanna sacrifice

01:08:14.077 --> 01:08:18.123
at the last ounce of kilowatts
that we can get from a dam

01:08:19.350 --> 01:08:23.320
but there are other ways we
can get much of our power

01:08:24.370 --> 01:08:27.160
without destroying the life world

01:08:27.160 --> 01:08:31.650
of a whole spectrum of
species that depend on it.

01:08:31.650 --> 01:08:35.750
And who knows what their
effects are in the long run,

01:08:35.750 --> 01:08:36.583
if they're lost.

01:08:40.080 --> 01:08:41.540
- Thanks, Jim.

01:08:41.540 --> 01:08:43.360
So we have a few more minutes

01:08:43.360 --> 01:08:45.410
and we have several more questions.

01:08:45.410 --> 01:08:48.350
I wanna ask Jim, do you mind continuing

01:08:48.350 --> 01:08:52.550
to answer questions for
another eight minutes or so?

01:08:52.550 --> 01:08:53.610
- [Jim] Sure, of course.

01:08:53.610 --> 01:08:57.680
- Okay, so the next
question from John Sidel,

01:08:57.680 --> 01:09:02.380
after John is Michael Vann, just let me,

01:09:02.380 --> 01:09:07.240
after John will be Michael
Vann, then Allen Potkin

01:09:07.240 --> 01:09:10.180
Jamie Anderson and Hans Steinmueller.

01:09:10.180 --> 01:09:13.350
So you could see there's a
fair number of questions,

01:09:13.350 --> 01:09:15.620
let's have John ask his

01:09:15.620 --> 01:09:18.113
and then we'll see how
far we get in that list.

01:09:19.160 --> 01:09:19.993
- Hi John.

01:09:20.890 --> 01:09:22.450
- Hi, Jim, nice to see you.

01:09:22.450 --> 01:09:26.120
You're the last person I've
had an indoor meal with

01:09:26.120 --> 01:09:27.664
other than my family

01:09:27.664 --> 01:09:32.664
- (laughs) On March 16th or something.

01:09:32.930 --> 01:09:33.763
- Yeah, exactly.

01:09:35.130 --> 01:09:37.260
Brilliant talk, wonderful link

01:09:37.260 --> 01:09:40.280
to get a sense of the forthcoming book.

01:09:40.280 --> 01:09:42.410
And one thing I was struck by

01:09:42.410 --> 01:09:45.820
was the sort of characteristic reference

01:09:45.820 --> 01:09:49.700
to a variety of different rivers

01:09:49.700 --> 01:09:52.520
and commonalities across them.

01:09:52.520 --> 01:09:55.970
But I guess my question is
whether there reaches a point

01:09:55.970 --> 01:09:59.370
in human history, recent history,

01:09:59.370 --> 01:10:03.620
where we find that what
happens in one river,

01:10:03.620 --> 01:10:04.880
happens in other rivers

01:10:04.880 --> 01:10:08.210
not only simultaneously more or less,

01:10:08.210 --> 01:10:10.940
but in an interconnected fashion.

01:10:10.940 --> 01:10:13.940
So for example, we know from Mark Elvin,

01:10:13.940 --> 01:10:18.940
what happens to the Pearl River
Delta with silt cultivation

01:10:22.670 --> 01:10:24.470
but then that has knock on effects

01:10:24.470 --> 01:10:29.470
in terms of stimulating demand
for rice from elsewhere.

01:10:31.410 --> 01:10:34.570
So this then impacts on
the Mekong, arguably maybe,

01:10:34.570 --> 01:10:36.590
in the Red River Delta as well.

01:10:36.590 --> 01:10:39.490
And it could even be that if
you go back to the conditions

01:10:41.453 --> 01:10:45.410
that stimulate French
demand for Chinese silk

01:10:45.410 --> 01:10:48.350
that they come from things
that happened in rivers

01:10:48.350 --> 01:10:51.210
the (indistinct), the
Rhone in France, who knows,

01:10:51.210 --> 01:10:53.050
it might be there, I'm not sure.

01:10:53.050 --> 01:10:55.040
But in other words,
does there come a point

01:10:55.040 --> 01:10:58.410
where what happens in one river,

01:10:58.410 --> 01:11:01.330
happens in all rivers or
that that interconnectedness

01:11:01.330 --> 01:11:03.980
with steamboats and otherwise takes on

01:11:03.980 --> 01:11:08.980
a kind of a catalytic effect,
kind of multiplier effect

01:11:10.030 --> 01:11:11.450
so you can't study one river

01:11:11.450 --> 01:11:14.910
without running into the
impact of other rivers

01:11:14.910 --> 01:11:18.113
that the histories are
intertwined as it were.

01:11:22.520 --> 01:11:26.470
- That's a rich question and could go

01:11:26.470 --> 01:11:31.463
in many different directions
in terms of talking about it.

01:11:33.230 --> 01:11:38.060
It seems to me, it's useful actually

01:11:38.060 --> 01:11:43.060
to once you get steam power
and you get these enormous

01:11:45.410 --> 01:11:50.410
sort of barges that can
carry huge bulk goods

01:11:50.940 --> 01:11:53.590
on navigable rivers, like the Mississippi

01:11:53.590 --> 01:11:56.290
and the Rhine and so
on, and they're designed

01:11:56.290 --> 01:12:00.310
and engineered or transformed
for exactly that purpose.

01:12:00.310 --> 01:12:05.310
They carry both goods,
especially they are superior

01:12:06.460 --> 01:12:09.380
to any other form of transportation
where that's available

01:12:11.460 --> 01:12:15.750
for all goods that have
a relatively low value

01:12:16.730 --> 01:12:18.940
per unit weight and volume.

01:12:18.940 --> 01:12:22.870
So that, for example, that's
why the lower Mississippi

01:12:22.870 --> 01:12:25.990
is the cotton cultivation, right?

01:12:25.990 --> 01:12:29.710
And its consequences,
the shipping of grain

01:12:31.372 --> 01:12:32.720
and of course gravel and stone

01:12:32.720 --> 01:12:36.613
and charcoal and timber, et cetera.

01:12:37.640 --> 01:12:39.930
And so somehow it seems to me

01:12:39.930 --> 01:12:44.930
that both the invention of steam power

01:12:46.210 --> 01:12:51.210
and the capacity to move
an enormous amount of cargo

01:12:52.920 --> 01:12:54.410
with relatively little friction

01:12:54.410 --> 01:12:57.931
because that's what the
river provides is important.

01:12:57.931 --> 01:13:02.931
But it seems to me that our
capacity to change the rivers,

01:13:06.110 --> 01:13:11.070
that's why I went out of my
way to emphasize dynamite

01:13:11.070 --> 01:13:15.165
and reinforce concrete
an earth moving machinery

01:13:15.165 --> 01:13:20.130
because our capacity to make rivers

01:13:20.130 --> 01:13:25.130
do what we want them to do
in order to serve as vehicles

01:13:25.810 --> 01:13:27.833
commerce or transportation

01:13:27.833 --> 01:13:31.220
are dependent on those late 19th century

01:13:31.220 --> 01:13:33.160
and early 20th century.

01:13:33.160 --> 01:13:38.160
And let me also add, Ian
would know this, and many

01:13:39.900 --> 01:13:44.450
of you would know this from
work on Asia, that in fact

01:13:44.450 --> 01:13:46.140
and it's not just the Chinese,

01:13:46.140 --> 01:13:48.063
but it's especially the Chinese,

01:13:49.210 --> 01:13:52.570
there is a dam industrial complex

01:13:53.830 --> 01:13:56.603
in which having dammed
every river in China,

01:13:57.940 --> 01:14:00.220
all of these engineers on the capital

01:14:00.220 --> 01:14:04.393
are looking to build dams in
Southeast Asia, in Africa,

01:14:05.350 --> 01:14:09.450
just the way the Italians and
Norwegians wanna build tunnels

01:14:09.450 --> 01:14:10.713
everywhere on the world.

01:14:11.610 --> 01:14:16.610
So there is, if you like,
there's an enormous emphasis

01:14:16.900 --> 01:14:20.360
because you can prove
certain benefits are there.

01:14:20.360 --> 01:14:24.380
We could go into the cost
benefit analysis that is so bogus

01:14:24.380 --> 01:14:29.380
with respect to the
organization, the rationale

01:14:31.280 --> 01:14:35.303
for dams in terms of
their economic benefits.

01:14:36.330 --> 01:14:39.740
But it seems to me that
there is, if you like,

01:14:39.740 --> 01:14:44.330
a kind of international
combine of engineers

01:14:44.330 --> 01:14:48.170
major corporations, state,
and non-state corporations

01:14:48.170 --> 01:14:53.170
for whom river engineering
is their business

01:14:54.150 --> 01:14:58.170
and they have an enormous
amount of influence,

01:14:58.170 --> 01:15:01.730
it provides jobs, benefits,
capital, and so on,

01:15:02.720 --> 01:15:05.593
and so that shouldn't be
overlooked, I don't think.

01:15:10.240 --> 01:15:11.240
- Thanks, Jim.

01:15:11.240 --> 01:15:14.070
Now we have a question from Michael Vann.

01:15:14.070 --> 01:15:18.650
- Hi, thank you so much for
the great talk Professor Scott

01:15:18.650 --> 01:15:19.483
a big fan of your work.

01:15:19.483 --> 01:15:22.060
Also, thank you to Yale
for making this public.

01:15:22.060 --> 01:15:24.660
This is really the Zoom
or the COVID silver lining

01:15:24.660 --> 01:15:27.440
for a lot of us is the Zoom talks.

01:15:27.440 --> 01:15:30.160
Just real quick question,
if the answer is no,

01:15:30.160 --> 01:15:32.715
move on to the next
question, but if it's, yes,

01:15:32.715 --> 01:15:35.620
I'd like to hear a
little more, do you find,

01:15:35.620 --> 01:15:40.620
in your analysis of the rivers,
the term Anthropocene useful

01:15:40.700 --> 01:15:43.890
for categorizing historical
development, if not fine,

01:15:43.890 --> 01:15:48.820
but if yes, what does the
study of rivers tell us

01:15:48.820 --> 01:15:52.033
about conceptualizing the Anthropocene?

01:15:55.420 --> 01:16:00.080
- Unfortunately, the answer
is yes, that is to say

01:16:00.080 --> 01:16:01.730
now I have to elaborate, don't I?

01:16:07.130 --> 01:16:12.130
Well, one of the ways in
which I understand this

01:16:15.140 --> 01:16:19.660
is to take George Perkins
Marsh's book from 1864.

01:16:28.560 --> 01:16:31.000
And so he's operating at a time,

01:16:31.000 --> 01:16:35.440
before at least the real
growth of steam power

01:16:37.410 --> 01:16:41.840
and he understands the way in
which deforestation and so on.

01:16:41.840 --> 01:16:45.490
So it's what I would describe

01:16:46.460 --> 01:16:51.460
as a lighter, thin Anthropocene
that he's describing

01:16:52.010 --> 01:16:54.960
in the way in which we've changed rivers.

01:16:54.960 --> 01:16:55.850
And it seems to me

01:16:55.850 --> 01:17:00.240
that because of the
technological innovations

01:17:00.240 --> 01:17:04.320
that I mentioned with the
dynamite, a reinforced concrete,

01:17:04.320 --> 01:17:09.320
et cetera, is that our
capacity to intervene

01:17:09.430 --> 01:17:13.060
and reshape the landscape
and the hydrology of rivers

01:17:14.170 --> 01:17:16.263
has grown at an exponential rate.

01:17:17.170 --> 01:17:20.330
And that as a result

01:17:20.330 --> 01:17:23.840
it's become an important
political resource

01:17:23.840 --> 01:17:25.730
for lots of States.

01:17:25.730 --> 01:17:30.540
I think the Burmese military,

01:17:30.540 --> 01:17:34.280
as a specialist are building
roads and dams and bridges,

01:17:34.280 --> 01:17:37.553
basically that's all it
does as near as I can tell,

01:17:37.553 --> 01:17:42.110
so like an engineering,
military regime say,

01:17:42.110 --> 01:17:43.430
as if the Army Corps of Engineers

01:17:43.430 --> 01:17:45.463
took over the United States.

01:17:46.740 --> 01:17:51.733
But it seems to me that yes, I
mean, it's hard to understand

01:17:56.080 --> 01:17:57.140
what's happened to rivers

01:17:57.140 --> 01:18:02.140
without these anthropogenic interventions.

01:18:02.330 --> 01:18:07.330
And the fact which I
think is often overlooked,

01:18:07.690 --> 01:18:10.360
is that in 1750,

01:18:10.360 --> 01:18:13.950
there were only three
quarters of a billion of us

01:18:13.950 --> 01:18:16.303
and now we're going on 8 billion.

01:18:17.380 --> 01:18:21.377
So it's not as if you know the arthropods

01:18:25.350 --> 01:18:27.670
that are screwing up the planet

01:18:27.670 --> 01:18:32.607
are eight times more numerous
than they were in 1750.

01:18:35.450 --> 01:18:40.450
So that all by itself
is an enormous burden

01:18:40.900 --> 01:18:43.443
on the resources that a river can provide.

01:18:48.810 --> 01:18:51.710
- Thanks, given the time I'm gonna ask

01:18:51.710 --> 01:18:56.626
if Alan Potkin, Jamie
Anderson and Han Steinmueller

01:18:56.626 --> 01:19:00.030
might ask their questions together

01:19:00.030 --> 01:19:02.040
so that we can kind of see

01:19:02.040 --> 01:19:04.100
if there's a thread linking them together.

01:19:04.100 --> 01:19:05.540
I'm sorry to do that to all of you,

01:19:05.540 --> 01:19:09.350
I know you've been waiting
patiently, but it is 1:22.

