01:20:58.490 --> 01:20:59.323
Thanks.

01:21:00.240 --> 01:21:03.350
- I shall do just very briefly,

01:21:03.350 --> 01:21:06.140
I actually spent the better part

01:21:06.140 --> 01:21:10.230
of a week in the Mandalay
fish markets, right?

01:21:10.230 --> 01:21:15.230
Talking to old fish summers
and trying to figure out

01:21:17.316 --> 01:21:19.351
the naming of fishes and keeping all

01:21:19.351 --> 01:21:21.420
of this straight and an
enormously difficult thing,

01:21:21.420 --> 01:21:26.420
even if you're working in
English or French or on Burmese,

01:21:26.730 --> 01:21:30.500
and the names of all the
fish were unfamiliar to me.

01:21:30.500 --> 01:21:33.120
But it was clear to me
that there was actually

01:21:33.120 --> 01:21:35.060
a lot of the fish sold
in the Mandalay market

01:21:35.060 --> 01:21:39.541
are fish that come from the Delta

01:21:39.541 --> 01:21:43.230
and not from the river itself.

01:21:43.230 --> 01:21:46.460
So I think it's true that there is not

01:21:46.460 --> 01:21:49.820
a sort of huge fishing industry

01:21:49.820 --> 01:21:52.263
at least as I experienced it.

01:21:53.600 --> 01:21:58.600
I think that, well,
the question is whether

01:22:01.270 --> 01:22:05.890
the Myitson dam is, if you
wanna make an argument,

01:22:05.890 --> 01:22:08.663
in favor of hydroelectric power,

01:22:09.680 --> 01:22:10.960
and it's a plausible argument

01:22:10.960 --> 01:22:12.650
if you're concentrating on a homo sapiens

01:22:12.650 --> 01:22:13.800
as I mentioned earlier.

01:22:14.670 --> 01:22:19.670
Then it behooves us if
I stand in your shoes

01:22:20.870 --> 01:22:24.810
for the time being to figure
out how one can generate

01:22:25.770 --> 01:22:29.540
in a cost-effective way as
much hydroelectric power

01:22:29.540 --> 01:22:32.900
as possible with as little damage

01:22:32.900 --> 01:22:35.800
to the natural hydrology of a river

01:22:35.800 --> 01:22:38.950
and the lifeforms in the
river and the Myitsone Dam,

01:22:38.950 --> 01:22:43.483
I think was not an optimal
solution to that problem.

01:22:49.840 --> 01:22:50.720
- Go ahead, Jamie.

01:22:50.720 --> 01:22:52.310
- Okay, well, Professor Scott

01:22:52.310 --> 01:22:54.330
thank you for your wonderful talk.

01:22:54.330 --> 01:22:56.240
This is probably a big question

01:22:56.240 --> 01:23:01.240
but I'll just put it out
there, looking at the rivers

01:23:01.440 --> 01:23:04.900
prior to our ability to dam
up rivers or reroute rivers,

01:23:04.900 --> 01:23:08.340
so humankind's ability to
kind of alter the effect.

01:23:08.340 --> 01:23:12.240
Do you see the human
settlement along the Irrawaddy

01:23:12.240 --> 01:23:16.760
responded to these transition
between this kind of aquatic

01:23:16.760 --> 01:23:19.710
terrestrial transition zone
that you had talked about?

01:23:19.710 --> 01:23:22.950
Do you see, historically would
we look for the footprint

01:23:22.950 --> 01:23:25.870
of kind of human settlement in cycles,

01:23:25.870 --> 01:23:28.260
did they understand the
cycles and the changes,

01:23:28.260 --> 01:23:30.870
I'm thinking about the Red
River, but I was wondering

01:23:30.870 --> 01:23:33.720
how in the Irrawaddy, if you
saw any evidence of that.

01:23:33.720 --> 01:23:37.840
So when we actually look for
evidence of human settlement

01:23:37.840 --> 01:23:41.000
we should look in this kind
of patterns of river usage

01:23:41.000 --> 01:23:44.000
or the river itself, as opposed to-

01:23:44.000 --> 01:23:46.810
- It's a good point, my
understanding is that

01:23:46.810 --> 01:23:48.853
like for many other rivers,

01:23:50.280 --> 01:23:55.280
that if you're looking for
early human settlements,

01:23:56.820 --> 01:23:58.593
you're looking at,

01:23:59.641 --> 01:24:02.890
and that's true for many of
the Delta towns, by the way

01:24:03.920 --> 01:24:06.240
they happen to be on little bluffs

01:24:06.240 --> 01:24:11.240
that are above the floodplain
and relatively safe

01:24:11.930 --> 01:24:16.930
from the annual floods
and storms and so on.

01:24:19.784 --> 01:24:22.640
And I think that's true for
the upper Irrawaddy as well,

01:24:22.640 --> 01:24:25.980
I'm not as conversant with the archeology

01:24:25.980 --> 01:24:29.593
of the early settlements along
the Irrawaddy as I might be,

01:24:30.840 --> 01:24:34.740
but that's my impression
that you don't want to look,

01:24:34.740 --> 01:24:37.980
at the, if you like the, the floodplain

01:24:37.980 --> 01:24:42.410
is sort of rich floodplain
soils but you want to look

01:24:42.410 --> 01:24:46.230
to nearby and what we're
talking about things

01:24:46.230 --> 01:24:51.230
like things that are 20, 30 meters higher,

01:24:51.510 --> 01:24:56.290
or are no more often than
the surrounding floodplain,

01:25:00.140 --> 01:25:00.973
it's my guess.

01:25:03.960 --> 01:25:06.800
- Great, and Hans will
have the last question

01:25:06.800 --> 01:25:11.800
before we stop the recording
and then just we'll have

01:25:11.853 --> 01:25:13.760
a open few minutes together as well.

01:25:13.760 --> 01:25:14.603
Go ahead, Hans.

01:25:15.610 --> 01:25:19.380
- Thanks, hi, that was a
extremely inspiring talk,

01:25:19.380 --> 01:25:24.150
and my question is about complexity.

01:25:24.150 --> 01:25:27.470
I found, in general, I'm very sympathetic

01:25:27.470 --> 01:25:32.470
to this opposition between
the kind of simplification

01:25:33.310 --> 01:25:38.310
and leaving the river to its complexity,

01:25:38.390 --> 01:25:40.250
and that simplification in general

01:25:40.250 --> 01:25:42.820
had bad and negative consequences

01:25:42.820 --> 01:25:47.150
and little kind of prevention efforts

01:25:47.150 --> 01:25:50.740
created the possibility
for much bigger floods,

01:25:50.740 --> 01:25:53.780
but I'm wondering if this is always true

01:25:53.780 --> 01:25:58.600
and whether or not sometimes
we can also grant rivers

01:25:58.600 --> 01:26:02.120
as the possibility to recreate
new forms of complexity

01:26:02.120 --> 01:26:05.140
or to adapt to new situations.

01:26:05.140 --> 01:26:07.410
And one reference I had

01:26:07.410 --> 01:26:12.200
that was kind of the Rhine and Heidegger,

01:26:12.200 --> 01:26:17.130
because actually in this
essay on technology,

01:26:17.130 --> 01:26:22.130
which he wrote in the 1950s
the Rhine plays a crucial role.

01:26:22.180 --> 01:26:23.900
And specifically the opposition

01:26:23.900 --> 01:26:26.670
between the pre-rectified Rhine,

01:26:26.670 --> 01:26:29.780
where poets such as
Heidegger look at the Rhine

01:26:29.780 --> 01:26:34.780
as authentic kind of feeling
and engagement with the world,

01:26:36.130 --> 01:26:39.310
and then when he, in
1950, looks at the Rhine

01:26:39.310 --> 01:26:42.850
the only thing he sees is
dams, hydroelectric dams,

01:26:42.850 --> 01:26:44.640
and he looks at one specifically

01:26:44.640 --> 01:26:47.840
and says this is what modern
technology does to the world

01:26:47.840 --> 01:26:51.946
that makes the world
inauthentic unreal kind of.

01:26:51.946 --> 01:26:52.860
So it's like, like you said,

01:26:52.860 --> 01:26:54.550
you know that's where you cut off

01:26:54.550 --> 01:26:57.810
all the four legs of the cot
it's not a river any longer,

01:26:57.810 --> 01:27:00.800
it's something else,
but Heidegger assessment

01:27:00.800 --> 01:27:03.070
seems to be very black
and white in the sense

01:27:03.070 --> 01:27:06.050
that modern technology
totally destroys everything

01:27:06.050 --> 01:27:09.710
in that there's no more
complexity and it's inauthentic.

01:27:09.710 --> 01:27:11.860
And my question is whether or not

01:27:11.860 --> 01:27:13.863
this is kind of taking it too far.

01:27:18.010 --> 01:27:22.280
- The question is whether Heidegger's term

01:27:22.280 --> 01:27:25.653
inauthentic is the appropriate one to use.

01:27:27.320 --> 01:27:32.320
I mean, depending on the
perspective one might want to adopt

01:27:32.400 --> 01:27:35.100
and there are several
perspectives that are plausible.

01:27:37.050 --> 01:27:42.050
One might say that the river
was relatively impoverished,

01:27:45.400 --> 01:27:50.400
in the sense that its biodiversity
was drastically reduced,

01:27:53.351 --> 01:27:56.980
if you like the wetlands
that were if you like,

01:27:56.980 --> 01:28:00.550
the nursery beds of most of the life

01:28:01.713 --> 01:28:04.003
along the Rhine were destroyed.

01:28:06.614 --> 01:28:10.960
And I think if you want
to then like Heidegger,

01:28:10.960 --> 01:28:15.557
concentrate on the human
produced landscape of dams

01:28:17.370 --> 01:28:21.150
and levees and so on,
then you get if you like,

01:28:21.150 --> 01:28:24.990
the inauthentic
architectural interventions

01:28:24.990 --> 01:28:26.860
of homo sapiens, sort of,

01:28:26.860 --> 01:28:30.860
so I see why he wants to
call them inauthentic.

01:28:30.860 --> 01:28:35.080
I was impressed, this is
something of a slight diversion,

01:28:35.080 --> 01:28:40.010
but I was impressed with
the Japanese practice

01:28:41.280 --> 01:28:43.000
which was abandoned at some point

01:28:43.000 --> 01:28:46.170
but an older practice of river management

01:28:47.010 --> 01:28:52.010
in which Japanese river
managers, engineers

01:28:52.162 --> 01:28:57.162
would hypothecate a kind of
intervention in the river

01:28:58.790 --> 01:29:00.630
for human purposes,
they were not interested

01:29:00.630 --> 01:29:02.513
in the wildlife in particular.

01:29:04.380 --> 01:29:08.160
But they also understood
that they did not understand

01:29:08.160 --> 01:29:10.710
the behavior of the river entirely.

01:29:10.710 --> 01:29:15.590
And so what they would do
is to build a small weir

01:29:15.590 --> 01:29:19.140
where they would imagine they
would build a larger weir

01:29:20.680 --> 01:29:23.100
and they would build it and
then they would, if you like

01:29:23.100 --> 01:29:25.670
stand back for two or three years

01:29:25.670 --> 01:29:30.140
and see what the river did with
it, how it changed the flow,

01:29:30.140 --> 01:29:33.510
how everything changed around it,

01:29:33.510 --> 01:29:37.830
whether it accumulated silt,
what happened down river

01:29:37.830 --> 01:29:40.410
and up were from it and so on.

01:29:40.410 --> 01:29:42.500
And then they would in a sense,

01:29:42.500 --> 01:29:44.600
design their next intervention

01:29:44.600 --> 01:29:47.290
which could be the removal
of the weir for that matter,

01:29:47.290 --> 01:29:48.820
they would design their next intervention

01:29:48.820 --> 01:29:52.580
by what they had learned in the interim

01:29:52.580 --> 01:29:54.910
by not assuming that
they understood the river

01:29:54.910 --> 01:29:57.520
completely at the
beginning of the exercise.

01:29:57.520 --> 01:30:00.710
And I thought there's a kind of, I dunno,

01:30:00.710 --> 01:30:03.693
Daoist humility if you like to this way,

01:30:04.674 --> 01:30:09.560
of understanding how to an
engineer and landscape a river

01:30:09.560 --> 01:30:14.560
that sounds to me rather more
scientifically defensible

01:30:15.790 --> 01:30:18.700
given the facts that the hydrology,

01:30:18.700 --> 01:30:21.720
I mean the effort to
understand the hydrology

01:30:21.720 --> 01:30:25.750
of the lower Irrawaddy scientifically

01:30:25.750 --> 01:30:29.646
as the creation of islands in the Delta,

01:30:29.646 --> 01:30:31.780
there just too many variables

01:30:33.332 --> 01:30:38.332
to make it a scientific
formula and all the more reason

01:30:41.227 --> 01:30:44.880
to observe carefully what
happens at a particular instance,

01:30:44.880 --> 01:30:48.100
knowing that it's not
gonna be exactly replicated

01:30:48.100 --> 01:30:49.223
in the next instance.

01:30:53.160 --> 01:30:54.260
I hope that's helpful.

01:30:55.740 --> 01:30:56.713
- Thank you, Jim.

01:30:58.230 --> 01:31:00.930
I think we should give Jim
a little bit of a rest here

01:31:02.420 --> 01:31:06.050
and join me in thanking him
for such a fantastic talk

01:31:06.050 --> 01:31:07.613
and wonderful conversation.

01:31:08.470 --> 01:31:10.013
So thank you, Jim.

01:31:10.865 --> 01:31:12.365
- [Jim] Thank you.

